r"""THE DRAW CENSUS -- every read an effect's draws make of a texel PAGE's bytes, at the depth and
through the CLUT they read it at, on the screen and into the render target.  CHANNEL D's evidence.

WHY THE TEXEL LANE HAS TO KNOW
------------------------------
A repaint changes a record's bytes; what the game shows is whatever its draws make of them.  The static
derivation (``so`` records, the program's registrations) says how a cell OUGHT to be read, and for most
of the scenery surface it says nothing at all (``depth-unknown``).  Channel D started from the render
target's feeds (:mod:`ff9mapkit.summons.render_target`): the routes census that measured them ran
``--no-draw``, so it saw only the draws INTO the target, and it booked them per CELL.  This table is
the rest: the headless plugin's every frame drawn, on all 372 containers, and every fragment that
SAMPLED a texel booked --

* **against the RECORD whose bytes it sampled, not the cell.**  A cell is not a picture: several
  writers upload into one cell over a cast (the co-transform cells), and the program copies bytes from
  cell to cell (the scroll blits).  The census follows every halfword from the upload that put it in
  VRAM, through every copy, to the read -- so a read is charged to the container bytes it actually saw,
  and a page's depth evidence is the reads of THAT page's bytes, wherever in VRAM they were read.  A
  per-cell census mistakes two writers' bytes read at two depths for one picture read twice;
* **before the cutout test, and whether or not it changed a pixel.**  A depth must count where the
  page's texels happen to be clear, because a repaint is exactly what makes them not clear;
* **on the screen and into the target, apart**, with the screen fragments that changed a pixel kept
  beside the reads (``shown``) -- the evidence that the bytes reach the screen at all;
* **per CLUT**, so the palette the bytes are read through is a measurement, not a page-level join.

Bytes no container record supplied (VRAM's initial fill, the caster's common textures, data the program
builds) are booked against no page and are not in this table.  :func:`reads` is the table per page;
``render_target.drawn_depth`` folds it into the channel: a page whose bytes any draw reads at two depths
is the same bytes as two pictures, and never emits.

THE MEASUREMENT
---------------
``studies/custom-summons/headless/raster.py routes --draw`` (the user's own plugin, headless, every frame
rendered, the source tracker armed; SCRATCH ``headless/raster/screen``).  Calibrated against the static
derivation before it was trusted, and re-derivation-pinned: ``studies/custom-summons/tier-w/
screen_gates.py`` reads the census again and asserts equality row for row.  The counts are asserted AT
IMPORT, so a truncated table fails loudly.

The tracker is also what found that the kit's page map was wrong for a third of the scenery cells
(``reskin.scenery_pages``, THE UPLOAD WALK): charging reads to pages needs each page where the game loads
it, and 943 cells were not.  Gate C1 holds the page map to every measured upload.  On the fixed map the
census charges 2,598 (page, cell, depth) rows; 573,556,309 fragments read bytes no container upload
supplied (VRAM's fill, the caster's shared textures, the program's own), 81,203,770 container bytes no
scenery page holds (creature and other textures), and 199,204,812 bytes held by more than one page,
which are charged to each of them.

WHAT IT CANNOT SEE
------------------
:data:`CAVEAT`.  The headless stage is not a battle: no battle camera, the actors at the origin, the
live cast path only (Battle Speed < 3), and an opening caster burst no in-game capture has
(``studies/custom-summons/headless/README.md``; the burst draws the caster's common textures, which are
no page's bytes).  A draw on a frame the stage never reached, or one the real camera and targets would
change, is invisible to it.

PROVENANCE
----------
Effect ids, container offsets, VRAM coordinates, depths, CLUT cells and fragment counts.  No stock byte,
no hex run.
"""
